 "meta": {
  "type": "howto",
  "term": "Employee turnover rate",
  "title": "How a department store's HR team deals with staff who keep quitting: employee turnover rate",
  "slug": "howto-hr-turnover",
  "family_or_category": "management",
  "caption": "A store that averages 200 staff lost 120 in a year. 120 ÷ 200 = 60% turnover, high even for retail\n\nHR measures it, prices it at about $4,000 per replacement ($480,000 a year), finds the cause in exit interviews and fixes it, then tracks the rate every month\n\nTurnover rarely gets its own line on the income statement, but it costs real money\n\n#edlintics #business #management #humanresources",
  "caption_x": "How a department store's HR team deals with staff who keep quitting: measure, price, find the cause, fix and track #edlintics",
  "first_comment": "What makes people stay in a job longest, in your experience? 👇💬",
  "maths_check": "120 / 200 = 0.60 = 60% turnover. 120 x $4,000 = $480,000 a year. BLS JOLTS retail separations about 3.8-4.0% a month (about 45-48% a year), so 60% is high. $4,000 is conservative against SHRM cost per hire ($4,700-$5,475). See assets/renders/howto-reels/w46/definition-check.md item 3",
  "threadsTopic": "Business",
  "threadsReason": "Staff turnover is a general business and management topic; no HR community is joined, so Business is the closest topic",
